Fixed. Both failures had the same single cause.

## Root cause

skill_dir_escape in src/config_paths.rs still carried the positive control from the previous session — a first-line early return that neutered the guard:

```rust
) -> Option<SkillEscape> {
    return None; // NEUTERED POSITIVE CONTROL   // <-- left in place
    #[allow(unreachable_code)]
    match std::fs::canonicalize(base_dir) { ... }
```

With skill_dir_escape hard-returning None, partition_escaped_skills classified everything as inside the root, so:

• `escaping_project_skill_is_refused_while_in_project_siblings_still_load failed because ["escapee", "local"] came back instead of ["local"]`.
• `unresolvable_skill_dir_is_refused_not_assumed_safe` failed because the unresolvable dir was kept instead of refused.

This is exactly the restore half of the mutate→run→restore discipline the project's safety rules call out — the break half ran, the marker was left behind, and only these two tests named it.

## Fix

Removed the neutered return and its now-dead #[allow(unreachable_code)], restoring the real body (canonicalize → starts_with(canon_root) → None/Outside, Err → Unresolvable). Two lines deleted, nothing else changed. The tests were already correct and were not weakened.

## Verification (all green)

• `cargo fmt` — clean
• `cargo build` — ok
• `cargo test — **5709 passed; 0 failed; 2 ignored** (both previously failing config_paths tests now pass; the 32-test config_paths::tests` module is green)
• `cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings` — exit 0
• `cargo test --test module_size — 28 passed (no register drift; src/config_paths.rs` is 1512 lines, under the 2000 cap)
• No NEUTERED/SABOTAGE/DO NOT COMMIT markers remain anywhere in src/, tests/, or scripts/.

One thing worth flagging for the record: the working tree also contains a 27-line wiring change in src/cli.rs plus a ("src/cli.rs", 7299) register bump, even though the task file listed cli.rs as out of scope. I left it intact rather than reverting, because the guard's emission-point call site lives there and removing it would leave partition_escaped_skills/skill_escape_refusal_message used only by tests (a dead_code failure under -D warnings). The gate currently passes because the register line matches the file's actual length. If that file was not meant to be touched, that's a separate decision from the test failures I was asked to fix.
